    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Chibchan-speaking chiefdoms in Colombia's eastern highlands, led by the zipa of Bacatá in the south and the zaque of Hunza in the north.</li><li>They traded salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth and emeralds for gold, which their smiths cast into ornaments and votive figures offered in sacred lakes.</li><li>A small Spanish force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> conquered them in 1537–1540 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> in the zipas' lands.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A small gold raft found in a cave at Pasca in 1969 shows a Muisca chief and his attendants afloat, the kind of lake ceremony behind the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

